The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (ret >= 0 && ret != rq->bio.bi_iter.bi_size) {
    bio_advance(&rq->bio, ret);
    zero_fill_bio(&rq->bio);
}
Reason:
When vfs_iocb_iter_read() is interrupted by SIGKILL, it returns a short read (ret < requested size but >= 0). The old code advances the bio past the actually read bytes and then zero‑fills the remaining bio. `zero_fill_bio()` marks all folios in the bio as uptodate, even though the data for those folios was not actually read from the backing file. This incorrectly signals that the folios contain valid data, which can lead to data corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (ret >= 0 && ret != rq->bio.bi_iter.bi_size)
    ret = -EIO;
Reason:
Instead of zero‑filling the remaining part of the bio, the fix converts a short read into an I/O error by setting `ret = -EIO`. Later in the function, when `ret < 0`, the folios are marked as not uptodate via `erofs_onlinefolio_end(fi.folio, ret < 0, false)`, and the bio’s `bi_status` is set accordingly. This prevents unused folios from being incorrectly marked uptodate and avoids potential data corruption.

